00:15:25,160 --> 00:15:26,160
Yeah, they lost my luggage.

233
00:15:26,160 --> 00:15:27,160
I know.

234
00:15:27,160 --> 00:15:28,160
God damn it.

235
00:15:28,160 --> 00:15:29,160
Germany knows.

236
00:15:29,160 --> 00:15:30,160
Yeah.

237
00:15:30,160 --> 00:15:31,160
So, we're coming back?

238
00:15:31,160 --> 00:15:33,160
You're coming back? When's your next Germany gig?

239
00:15:33,160 --> 00:15:39,160
We are starting September 24th, I think.

240
00:15:39,160 --> 00:15:40,160
Something like that.

241
00:15:40,160 --> 00:15:44,160
End of September and October with Megahertz.

242
00:15:44,160 --> 00:15:45,160
Ah, yeah, right.

243
00:15:45,160 --> 00:15:47,160
Yeah, and all over Europe.

244
00:15:47,160 --> 00:15:52,160
And then there will be a little time off working on a new album.

245
00:15:52,160 --> 00:15:54,160
Now I have to finish it because I said it.

246
00:15:54,160 --> 00:15:57,160
You do. I have it on record here.

247
00:15:57,160 --> 00:15:58,160
Yeah, I know.

248
00:15:58,160 --> 00:16:01,400
I'll send it to you like weekly, like there you said it.

249
00:16:01,400 --> 00:16:04,000
It's about time, it's about time.

250
00:16:04,000 --> 00:16:07,340
I never force, you know, I just never force writing a new album.

251
00:16:07,340 --> 00:16:09,300
So it just, it have to be the right time.

252
00:16:09,300 --> 00:16:16,440
And we have, you know, Elliot and I have been writing, I don't know, it's a lot of songs.

253
00:16:16,440 --> 00:16:20,640
So we just kind of have to go through everything and kind of dial in what we want to finish.

254
00:16:20,640 --> 00:16:21,640
That's really...

255
00:16:21,640 --> 00:16:26,500
It's been more like a philosophy session of like, what is a good song?

256
00:16:26,500 --> 00:16:27,500
What is a good song?

257
00:16:27,500 --> 00:16:32,500
I'm looking forward to that and when it's going to be released.

258
00:16:32,500 --> 00:16:33,500
Okay, thanks.

259
00:16:33,500 --> 00:16:34,500
My pleasure.

260
00:16:34,500 --> 00:16:35,500
See you.

261
00:16:35,500 --> 00:16:36,500
Bye.

262
00:16:57,500 --> 00:17:00,500
Every day the brain does the same.

263
00:17:02,500 --> 00:17:05,500
A signal says it's all okay.

264
00:17:06,500 --> 00:17:10,500
We know it's not, it's all that we got.

265
00:17:11,500 --> 00:17:15,500
We're still alive, forgot how to live.

266
00:17:16,500 --> 00:17:20,500
Trying to hang on to this world.

267
00:17:20,500 --> 00:17:25,500
What would you like to see in the future?

268
00:17:25,500 --> 00:17:28,500
Cool, last question.

269
00:17:28,500 --> 00:17:32,500
I already have four minutes for one question.

270
00:17:32,500 --> 00:17:34,500
Then I have to think of five more.

271
00:17:34,500 --> 00:17:37,500
I talk very slowly and philosophize a lot.

272
00:17:37,500 --> 00:17:43,500
I think it's great what you're doing for the scene and that you've shown our scene outside.

273
00:17:43,500 --> 00:17:48,500
What would you like to give our Gothic scene for the future?

274
00:17:48,500 --> 00:17:52,500
I always have a little problem, I'm a little scared that it will get too old.

275
00:17:52,500 --> 00:17:56,500
Yes, so the problem is a good question for the end, because you can talk about it for a long time.

276
00:17:56,500 --> 00:18:01,500
And the thing is, first of all, we are at home in many different scenes.

277
00:18:01,500 --> 00:18:03,500
We are also somehow in the metal scene.

278
00:18:03,500 --> 00:18:05,500
Exactly, I'll see you on Wacken next week again.

279
00:18:05,500 --> 00:18:12,500
We are somehow mainstream, we are somehow glam, we feel totally at home on every Christopher Street Day.

280
00:18:12,500 --> 00:18:18,500
And that's where we actually come to what I think is very good, but what is a problem for many.

281
00:18:18,500 --> 00:18:25,500
Because if you are not just part of this one scene as part of a scene, then that is often a problem.

282
00:18:25,500 --> 00:18:31,500
And every scene comes with a certain dogma and certain rules and do's and don'ts.

283
00:18:31,500 --> 00:18:37,500
And that's where we come to exactly the problem that many, and that's not just in the black scene,

284
00:18:37,500 --> 00:18:43,740
but also in metal and also in rap and no matter where, as soon as you are too far out, also somewhere else,

285
00:18:43,740 --> 00:18:50,160
you are in the worst case a scene traitor or you are just not true or you don't really belong to it

286
00:18:50,160 --> 00:18:58,440
and it is mixed too much and softened and we are no longer just among us and so on and I have to say

287
00:18:58,440 --> 00:19:02,880
that is of course type dependent but I have never felt comfortable with this only we so among us

288
00:19:02,880 --> 00:19:07,200
Because that always limits everything else and I feel different every day.

289
00:19:07,200 --> 00:19:14,880
And look, I'm sitting here with my stuff and wearing my disfavored world hoodie from Swiss with great pride.

290
00:19:14,880 --> 00:19:16,800
And the others that I produce.

291
00:19:16,800 --> 00:19:24,440
Am I allowed to produce something like that as a singer of a gothic metal band, a punk rap troupe?

292
00:19:24,440 --> 00:19:27,480
That's where we come to the question. Is that still true?

293
00:19:27,480 --> 00:19:28,920
Is that still true? Is that still punk rock?

294
00:19:28,920 --> 00:19:33,920
Exactly, exactly, super, this song, is that still punk rock? Exactly, punk, exactly the same.

295
00:19:33,920 --> 00:19:34,920
And they have exactly the same problem.

296
00:19:34,920 --> 00:19:41,920
Swiss and the others are also exactly on this border between mainstream and underground and these are the same discussions in every scene.

297
00:19:41,920 --> 00:19:53,920
What I would wish for would be, if everyone says, we would like to be treated empathetically, be understood and somehow, even though we are outsiders, are still accepted.

298
00:19:53,920 --> 00:19:57,560
then you have to accept this acceptance from the inside.

299
00:19:57,560 --> 00:20:02,560
I think a scene can only develop if you don't hide what's new.

300
00:20:02,560 --> 00:20:04,560
And I have two more minutes.

301
00:20:04,560 --> 00:20:08,560
The thing is, what you hear very often is this thing,

302
00:20:08,560 --> 00:20:11,560
Gothic scene has no offspring, metal scene has no offspring.

303
00:20:11,560 --> 00:20:14,560
Yes, or just the audience.

304
00:20:14,560 --> 00:20:17,560
When you look around at a festival, at a classic, I have to say,

305
00:20:17,560 --> 00:20:20,560
it feels like it's getting older, especially on the WGT I noticed it again.

306
00:20:20,560 --> 00:20:25,560
That's exactly the thing. If you just say, we actually only want Sisters of Mercy and The Cure,

307
00:20:25,560 --> 00:20:29,560
and then new bands come that might be like that, then it means, no, it's a copy.

308
00:20:29,560 --> 00:20:34,560
But then bands come that are completely different, try to blow fresh air into the scene,

309
00:20:34,560 --> 00:20:39,560
and then it means, but that's not like The Cure. And that's where the problem comes in.

310
00:20:39,560 --> 00:20:43,560
And then it might be like that, maybe when all of this collapses,

311
00:20:43,560 --> 00:20:49,560
then maybe at some point the black scene 2.0 will emerge from itself.

312
00:20:49,560 --> 00:20:51,560
We can only hope.

313
00:20:51,560 --> 00:20:56,560
Exactly. I would just like to be open and not lose the fun in the matter.

314
00:20:56,560 --> 00:21:06,560
Because we don't wear black because we all want to die and are so unhappy, but because we feel so comfortable, because it's a positive feeling.

315
00:21:06,560 --> 00:21:15,560
I actually just wrote a song about it, about wearing this black for our next album, somewhere in 2025.

316
00:21:15,560 --> 00:21:22,560
You know it all from the shirt, the black is my happy color.

317
00:21:22,560 --> 00:21:28,560
What I want to say is that we are here in the black scene to have fun.

318
00:21:28,560 --> 00:21:31,560
And fun has a lot to do with openness.

319
00:21:31,560 --> 00:21:36,560
That's not my thing. I would like to be open.

320
00:21:36,560 --> 00:21:42,560
But are you allowed to be on the Wacken on 70,000 tons of metal if you are here on the black scene?

321
00:21:42,560 --> 00:21:44,560
I just do it.

322
00:21:44,560 --> 00:21:47,560
Yes, you do it. But are you confronted with something like that?

323
00:21:47,560 --> 00:21:50,560
That you jump too far between the chairs?

324
00:21:50,560 --> 00:21:53,560
Actually less, because I'm mainly in the fashion scene.

325
00:21:53,560 --> 00:21:56,560
That's even worse.

326
00:21:56,560 --> 00:21:59,560
Yes, but I apparently found the niche there.

327
00:21:59,560 --> 00:22:02,560
You see, you're not cool at all. You're just here because of fashion.

328
00:22:02,560 --> 00:22:03,560
Exactly.

329
00:22:03,560 --> 00:22:06,560
But it's an interesting discussion.

330
00:22:06,560 --> 00:22:09,560
You have to make sure that you also get comments like that.

331
00:22:09,560 --> 00:22:12,560
Actually, I have little problems with negative comments.

332
00:22:12,560 --> 00:22:14,560
That's mostly envy anyway.

333
00:22:14,560 --> 00:22:20,560
So the most important thing I've learned, maybe I've taken over that from myself for five seconds,

334
00:22:20,560 --> 00:22:26,560
is that everything you get in negative comments is actually just about the commentators.

335
00:22:26,560 --> 00:22:27,560
Yes, anyway.

336
00:22:27,560 --> 00:22:29,560
In any case, that's also true.

337
00:22:29,560 --> 00:22:30,560
All right. Thank you very much.

